[36m(TaskRunner pid=331456)[0m We factorize \(9!\) into its prime factors:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=331456)[0m \[ 9! = 2^7 \times 3^4 \times 5^1 \times 7^1 \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=331456)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=331456)[0m The positive divisors of \(9! \) that have a units digit of 1 are those numbers of the form \(2^a \times 3^b \times 5^c \times 7^d\) where \(0 \leq a \leq 7\), \(0 \leq b \leq 4\), \(0 \leq c \leq 1\), and \(0 \leq d \leq 1\). We need such divisors that have a units digit of 1.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=331456)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=331456)[0m We note that the units digit of a number is equivalent to its value modulo 10. Therefore, we need to consider the divisors modulo 10.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=331456)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=331456)[0m The only possible cases for the units digit of 1 occur when the divisor is congruent to 1 modulo 10. For \(d = 0\), the divisor is \(2^a \times 3^b\), and for \(d = 1\), the divisor is \(2^a \times 3^b \times 7\).
[36m(TaskRunner pid=331456)[0m 
